Another one of the fabulously detailed and complicated roguelikes,
Omega, went through this debacle in the last decade or so. In 1994,
Robert Rendell   released 0.80. Sometime later, William Tanksley
started a piecemeal rewrite of Omega, staying in C.  Tanksley and
perhaps others working with him made a bunch of interesting
improvements fairly quickly -- quickly by roguelike standards,
anyhow -- and in 1999 released a somewhat buggy 0.90.4prealpha to
show them off. Since then, at least four other languages were
introduced to "make things easier", including a miniature version of
scheme and some elisp macros. I'm not sure if they remain or not.
The last message in the omega-dev group was posted in December 2007;
it was around that point that I downloaded the current source, and
it actually seemed to compile. But there's obviously a total failure
of will to continue with that game.

Now, Omega never had to begin with anything like the following that
Nethack does, obviously. Still, if you start moving the languages
around, you'd better have an awful lot of motivation to continue
with the project, or it has a damn good chance of dying a horrible
lingering death.



